#edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050145 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050145 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186184194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050145 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186184195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050145 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186184208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050145 #edb/ref 1031 "The Sample Variance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050145 #edb/ref 1033 3820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050145 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "30992ddf-ba31-551a-9df1-d2e25d94df94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050911 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050911 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050911 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050911 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050911 #edb/ref 1031 "The Sample Covariance Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050911 #edb/ref 1033 3138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050911 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "70a63868-505f-50a9-bb34-96c96024a1cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183829 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186183830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183829 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186183831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183829 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186183832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183829 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186183833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183829 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186183834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183829 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183829 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183829 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cf68f810-83c4-5c31-ab5d-3a95adffba29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183830 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183830 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183830 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ba80ffcf-73eb-5f1a-8fd4-d63450f1bfa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183831 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183831 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183831 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e450345a-e368-5b38-aab7-b820247601a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183832 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183832 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183832 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e8b6517e-8f2a-5f23-8e22-b1018a73b77c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183833 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183833 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183833 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18814d35-1a72-50a7-a1ca-7d133e3fff5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183834 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183834 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186183834 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "67109040-4d97-57d2-9184-2956dfd755e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184092 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184092 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$5, \\quad 3, \\quad 6, \\quad 4, \\quad 7$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184092 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a50a0dd2-10b1-5984-ae1b-6198de3f44ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184092 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$. Then the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{n}{n-1} \\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left(\\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right]\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- the sample variance $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance $\\sigma^2$, and\n- $\\hat{\\sigma}^2 = s^2$ denotes the unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2$ computed from the sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$.\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overline{x^2} - \\left(\\overline{x} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n$x^(―)|= \\frac{1}{5}(5 + 3 + 6 + 4 + 7); = \\frac{25}{5}; = 5$.\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n$x^{2}^(―)|= \\frac{1}{5}(5^{2} + 3^{2} + 6^{2} + 4^{2} + 7^{2}); = \\frac{135}{5}; = 27$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}^(―) - (x^(―))^{2} &= 27 - 5^{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^(^)^{2} &= s^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{n}{n - 1}[x^{2}^(―) - (x^(―))^{2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{5 - 1} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $2.5$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184093 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7881" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184093 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_9$ is a random sample of size $n=9$ from a population, and\n$\\overline{x^2} - \\left(\\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 4$.\n\nCompute an unbiased estimate of the population variance." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184093 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86da2f2b-8028-5807-ba02-63722c4c0f61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184093 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$. Then the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{n}{n-1} \\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left(\\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right]\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- the sample variance $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance $\\sigma^2$, and\n- $\\hat{\\sigma}^2 = s^2$ denotes an unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2$ that's computed from the sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$.\n\nHere, there are $n=9$ data points in the sample, and we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{x^2} - \\left(\\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^(^)^{2} &= s^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{n}{n - 1}[x^{2}^(―) - (x^(―))^{2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{9 - 1} \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{8} \\\\\n&= 4.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184112 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184112 #edb/ref 1071 "A random sample of size $n=81$ is conducted from a population. It is known that\n\n$\\sum_{i=1}^{81} \\left(x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 180$\n\nwhere each $x_i$ is a sample observation. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population standard deviation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184112 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3c0e272-0059-59e6-b3ea-55aaecb14cda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184112 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$. Then the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{n}{n-1} \\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left(\\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right]\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- the sample variance $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance $\\sigma^2$, and\n- $\\hat{\\sigma}^2 = s^2$ denotes an unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2$ that's computed from the sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$.\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{81} \\left(x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 180\n$$\n\nand $n=81$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^(^)^{2} &= s^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n - 1}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{81 - 1} \\cdot 180 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{80} \\cdot 180 \\\\\n&= 2.25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\hat{\\sigma}^2 = 2.25\n$$\n\nWe take the square root to compute an unbiased estimate of the standard deviation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\hat{\\sigma} = \\sqrt{2.25} = 1.5\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184194 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184194 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184194 #edb/ref 1039 "Estimating the Population Variance From Sample Data" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184194 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184194 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "897ae010-a9a2-5971-a1ec-c2618cb8ad2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184195 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184195 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184195 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing an Estimate of the Population Variance Using the Alternative Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184195 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184195 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cb182688-828c-5252-8d6d-d09c93ba2bc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184208 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184208 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184208 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184208 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184208 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing an Estimate of the Population Variance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184208 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184208 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2aa1599e-b9c0-5282-ac58-b10211cb7d30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184405 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184405 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184405 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184405 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184405 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184405 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184405 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184405 #edb/ref 1045 "The Sample Variance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184405 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186185039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184405 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184405 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "42cca35e-3169-59f3-9c38-76ccaaa1b972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184870 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184870 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88857" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184870 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$4, 8, 10, 6, 7$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184870 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0fd4e806-e213-5cdb-aa92-418bede47190" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184870 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184966 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184966 #edb/ref 1061 29345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184966 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d795603a-bac4-5e36-9889-7bf6375caf7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184966 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184967 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184967 #edb/ref 1061 29346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184967 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9f5deae4-70cb-564f-91d8-87a4b24e70ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184967 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184981 #edb/ref 1061 36799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184981 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ce2c432-29aa-5494-afaf-43ec20ee1b01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184981 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184988 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184988 #edb/ref 1061 37321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184988 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "84481413-cf20-5646-8866-e93e72e6dd48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184988 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185039 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185039 #edb/ref 1061 38668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185039 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "775ba336-e81a-5214-bcb3-13cce92d553e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185039 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185040 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185040 #edb/ref 1061 38675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185040 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "db6591d9-519a-5e8b-9a10-89dc5a7c761d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185040 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185326 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that for a sample of size $n$, the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2\n$$\n\nWe will now prove that $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2$, that is,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[S^2\\right] = \\sigma^2\n$$\n\nA major step towards this goal is to find the expected value of the sum of squares term:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E \\left[ \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 \\right] \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFirst, consider the following sum of squares:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this term by adding and subtracting the sample mean $\\overline X$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\left((X_i - \\overline X) + (\\overline X - \\mu)\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSquaring and applying the laws of summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)((X_{i} - X^(―)) + (X^(―) - μ))^{2} &=  \\\\\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)((X_{i} - X^(―))^{2} + 2(X_{i} - X^(―))(X^(―) - μ) + (X^(―) - μ)^{2}) &=  \\\\\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))^{2} + 2∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))(X^(―) - μ) + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X^(―) - μ)^{2} &=  \\\\\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))^{2} + 2(X^(―) - μ)∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―)) + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X^(―) - μ)^{2} &=  \\\\\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))^{2} + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X^(―) - μ)^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))^{2} + n(X^(―) - μ)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have used the fact that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i - \\overline X) = 0\n$$\n\nand that $(\\overline X - \\mu)$ does not depend on $i$. Thus, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2 = \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 + n(\\overline X- \\mu)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 = \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2 - n(\\overline X- \\mu)^2. \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast\\ast)\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side is the summation term $(\\ast)$ we wish to find the expectation of.\n\nNow, we make use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[(X_{i} - μ)^{2}] &= σ^{2} \\\\\nE[(X^(―) - μ)^{2}] &= Var[X^(―)] = \\frac{σ^{2}}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinding the expectation of $(\\ast\\ast)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))^{2}] &= E[∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - μ)^{2}] - nE[(X^(―) - μ)^{2}] \\\\\n&= nσ^{2} - n \\cdot \\frac{σ^{2}}{n} \\\\\n&= nσ^{2} - σ^{2} \\\\\n&= (n - 1)σ^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nE[\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))^{2}] = ((n - 1)σ^{2})/(n)\n$$\n\nThis tells us that the statistic\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2\n$$\n\nis a *biased* estimate of $\\sigma^2$. Therefore, to form an unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2$, we must divide our summation term by $n-1$, not $n$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[S^{2}] &= E[\\frac{1}{n - 1}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X_{i} - X^(―))^{2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n - 1} \\cdot (n - 1)σ^{2} \\\\\n&= σ^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185326 #edb/ref 1065 6032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185326 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bc43298c-5d5c-5750-8ac9-a9518e92f1bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185326 #edb/ref 1067 "Proof That the Sample Variance Is an Unbiased Estimator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185357 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$.\n\nWe've already seen that the sample mean $\\overline{X}$, given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nis an *unbiased* estimator of $\\mu$, which means that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\nThe definition of the sample mean follows naturally from the definition of $\\mu$.\n\nHowever, when constructing estimators for the population variance $\\sigma^2$, things are not so simple!\n\nFrom the definition of the population variance, you might expect that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nis an *unbiased* estimator for $\\sigma^2$. However, it can be shown that it is a *biased* estimator of $\\sigma^2$, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\\right] \\neq \\sigma^2\n$$\n\nThis might seem surprising, but it's true.\n\nTo compute an *unbiased* estimate of $\\sigma^2$, we use the so-called **sample variance**. The sample variance is denoted $S^2$, and is defined as\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nAt the end of this lesson, we will prove that $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Notice that instead of dividing by $n$ (as with our original biased estimator), we instead divide by $n-1$. This is sometimes referred to as **Bessel's correction**, as it corrects the bias of the original estimator.\n- We should think of $S^2$ as a random variable whose value varies according to the particular sample under consideration.\n- Since $S^2$ is a random variable, it has a sampling distribution (i.e., a probability distribution). We'll learn more about the sampling distribution of $S^2$ in future lessons.\n\nFinally, we have an alternative formula for the sample variance that's often more convenient in practice:\n\n$$\nS^2 =\\dfrac{n}{n-1}\\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left(\\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right]\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185357 #edb/ref 1065 6392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185357 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e496ddcf-d3f6-5835-b2cf-41a60fd869aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185357 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185358 #edb/ref 1064 "Here, we will show that\n\n$$\nS^2 =\\dfrac{n}{n-1}\\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left(\\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right]\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of $S^2$:\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses and distributing the summation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{2} &= \\frac{1}{n - 1}∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{2} - 2X_{i}X^(―) + (X^(―))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n - 1}[∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{2} - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)2X_{i}X^(―) + ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(X^(―))^{2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n - 1}[∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{2} - 2X^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i} + (X^(―))^{2} \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(n)1]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now consider each sum separately:\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2 = n\\cdot \\overline{X^2}$ follows directly from the definition of the mean of $X^2$\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i = n\\cdot \\overline{X}$ follows directly from the definition of the mean of $X$\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n 1 = \\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots+1}_{n\\, \\textrm{times}} = n$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^{2} &= \\frac{1}{n - 1}[∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}^{2} - 2X^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i} + (X^(―))^{2} \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(n)1] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n - 1}[n \\cdot X^{2}^(―) - 2X^(―) \\cdot n \\cdot X^(―) + (X^(―))^{2} \\cdot n] \\\\\n&= \\frac{n}{n - 1}[X^{2}^(―) - 2(X^(―))^{2} + (X^(―))^{2}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{n}{n - 1}[X^{2}^(―) - (X^(―))^{2}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185358 #edb/ref 1065 6397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185358 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "76207c26-f399-5ea4-aec8-b8b994fd8915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185358 #edb/ref 1067 "Proof of the Alternative Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
